Near Infrared Excitation and Emission in Rare Earth MOFs via Encapsulation of Organic Dyes
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x
www.rsc.org/
We successfully demonstrate that metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can be designed to be excited and emit within the biological diagnostic window \((650{-}1450\ \mathsf{n m})\) . An isoreticular series of anionic rare earth MOFs with fcu topology was synthesized using 10 different rare earth elements \((\mathsf{Y}^{3+},\,\mathsf{E u}^{3+},\,\mathsf{G d}^{3+},\,\mathsf{T b}^{3+},\,\mathsf{D y}^{3+},\,\mathsf{H o}^{3+},\,\mathsf{E r}^{3+},\,\mathsf{T r h}^{3+},\,\mathsf{Y b}^{3+},\,\mathsf{L u}^{3+})\) and common linear ditopic ligands. Five different cationic dye molecules were incorporated into the MOFs via ion exchange. When LDS 750, which exhibits low-energy absorption in the range \(450{-}770\ \mathsf{n m}\) , is loaded into a \(\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}\) -MOF, it can be used as an antenna to sensitize the near-infrared (NIR) emission of \(\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}\) centred at 980 nm.
## Introduction
Biological tissues exhibit low autofluorescence and low absorption in the so-called biological diagnostic window (650– \(1450\;\mathsf{n m})\) ). Biological imaging agents that both absorb and emit in this biological window can allow achievement of (i) higher sensitivity because their signals can be easily discriminated from the background and (ii) deeper tissue penetration due to minimal or no overlap of their excitation window with tissue absorption.1-3
## Results and discussion
Results and discussionAn isoreticular family of anionic fcu MOFs based on \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) clusters was selected for the present study.18 Pioneered by Eddaoudi and co-workers, these MOFs are typically constructed using monofluorinated dicarboxylates, which direct the formation of the \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) clusters.19-22 Here, we used 2,6-difluorobenzoic acid (DFBA) as a modulator to effect formation of the clusters and analogous fcu MOFs using nonfluorinated ligands including 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{-N D C})\), \(^{1,1^{\prime}}\)-biphenyl- \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\)-dicarboxylic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{-B P D C})\), and \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\)-azobenzenedicarboxylic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{-A B D C})\) (Fig. 1), namely \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-NDC, \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-BPDC, and \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-ABDC. These modulated MOF syntheses yielded large single crystals suitable for single crystal X-ray diffraction (SC-XRD) (Fig. S1), and structures of the \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-MOFs were successfully determined (Fig. 1c-e). Phase purity of the as-synthesized \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-MOFs was assessed and confirmed by comparing simulated diffraction patterns to those collected via powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Fig. S5). The chemical formulae of the as-synthesized MOFs were determined by a combination of elemental microanalysis and thermogravimetric analysis (see SI section 2.8 for details). It is noteworthy that the as-synthesized MOFs do not contain DFBA modulator, as determined via \({}^{1}\mathsf{H}\) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy of dissolved MOF samples (Fig. S9). Although activation procedures were not optimized, \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption experiments revealed that \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-NDC, \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-BPDC, and \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-ABDC were each permanently porous (Fig. S10).DFBA-modulated conditions were found to be compatible with 9 other \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) \((\mathsf{Y}^{3+},\,\mathsf{E u}^{3+},\,\mathsf{G d}^{3+},\,\mathsf{T h}^{3+},\,\mathsf{H o}^{3+},\,\mathsf{F r}^{3+},\,\mathsf{T m}^{3+},\,\mathsf{Y b}^{3+},\,\mathsf{L u}^{3+})\) in addition to \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\). Isostructural MOFs were formed in pure phases, as determined by comparing their respective PXRD patterns with those of \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-MOFs and simulated patterns from SC-XRD structures (Fig. S11-S13). Such results confirm that the synthesized MOFs are a versatile platform for the development of luminescent materials with different functional properties.The fcu \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\)-MOFs are anionic with dimethylammonium cations residing in the pores balancing the framework charges (see SI section 2.8 for details). Therefore, it is expected that the native cations can be exchanged with other positively charged species. In addition to the MOF ligands, a broader sensitization of the \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) centres could be obtained if cationic dyes absorbing at longer wavelengths were incorporated into the MOF channels. Ion exchange experiments were carried out with \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-NDC, \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-BPDC, and \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-ABDC, each having different pore dimensions, and five cationic dyes (Rhodamine 590, Rhodamine 610, Rhodamine 640, LDS 698, LDS 750). Space filling models of \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-BPDC and the five dyes are shown in Fig. 2a and b. After similar treatment with various dye solutions (Fig. S14), the MOF crystallinity was retained in all cases, as indicated by PXRD patterns (Fig. S15-S17). Different degrees of loading (Fig. 2c) were observed in \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-NDC, \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-BPDC, and \(\mathsf{D y}^{3+}\)-ABDC, as determined from \({}^{1}\mathsf{H}\) NMR analysis of the dissolved products (Fig. S18-S31).Dye-loaded \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\)-BPDC was chosen for luminescence studies because it could adsorb a quantifiable amount of dye
