Dye-loaded \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) -BPDC  was  chosen  for  luminescence studies because it could adsorb a quantifiable amount of dye
and because the dye molecules remain within the MOF channels after several cycles of washing. In contrast, only trace amounts of dyes could be incorporated into \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\)-NDC and significant quantities of dyes leached out of \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\)-ABDC upon washing, which could be due to unfavourable interactions between the dyes and the \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\)-ABDC MOFs. As previously articulated, having both antennae absorption and \(\mathsf{R E}^{3+}\) emission in the biological diagnostic window is ideal for optical imaging experiments and diagnostics on biological materials, especially when carried out in vivo. On the basis of this criterion, LDS 750 was chosen as the sensitizer and was incorporated into \(\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}\)-BPDC. The ratio of \(\mathsf{Y b}^{3+}\) to LDS 750 can be finely controlled by varying the loading condition (e.g. concentration, reaction time), as confirmed by \({}^{1}\mathsf{H}\) NMR quantification (Fig. S32) and summarized in Table S4.
